  "report_text": "# Personalization as a retail sales lever\n## Prepared for Michael Lee, Sales Team Lead\n\n### Executive summary\nRetail personalization can support sales by making product discovery more relevant, adding complementary items to a basket, prompting timely replenishment, and giving customers a consistent reason to return across store, web, app, and delivery. Public studies show that consumers say they value personalized experiences, while grocery and retailer examples illustrate recommendation and loyalty mechanics. These findings support testing personalization; they do not establish a reliable sales uplift for Lee’s Market.\n\nLee’s has a useful starting point: a Sales Strategy message reports that about 85% of transactions were linked to loyalty accounts. The message has a date inconsistency, and a separate internal document reports 45% engagement with product recommendations and a 25% AOV increase without a period, baseline, sample size, or method. Treat both as leads to verify, not as a forecast or proven causal result. [4][5]\n\n### How personalization can drive sales\n1. **Increase conversion by reducing choice friction.** Rank products, recipes, and offers using each shopper’s own browsing and purchase history, stated preferences, and the selected store’s assortment. Grocery personalization research describes recommendations and offers tailored to individual needs; a dunnhumby article reports that 71% of surveyed Singaporean shoppers said they were more likely to buy from brands that treated them as individuals. That is a directional signal from a different market, not a Lee’s forecast. [2]\n2. **Grow basket size with relevant complements.** Suggest items that complete a meal or shopping mission, such as hot-pot ingredients or a barbecue bundle, when a shopper has shown interest in those products. Make the offer useful and available, and measure the additional margin after discounts. Do not assume a customer wants a cuisine or product because of their ethnicity.\n3. **Prompt repeat visits with useful replenishment and loyalty offers.** Use purchase cadence only where the customer has opted into relevant communications; test reminders and value offers against a holdout. Keep routine discounts narrow enough to avoid subsidizing purchases that would have happened anyway.\n4. **Connect the experience across channels.** Carry customer-selected preferences and eligible offers consistently between Lee’s website, app, store checkout, and delivery. Supermarket News describes Tesco’s Clubcard Challenges as individualized purchase thresholds delivered to more than 10 million UK customers; the article does not quantify sales lift, so this is a design example rather than proof of expected performance at Lee’s. [3]\n\nA Deloitte article reports that 80% of surveyed consumers preferred brands offering personalized experiences and reported spending 50% more with them. It also reports that 92% of retailers believed they personalized effectively, while 48% of consumers agreed. These are survey findings with limited methodology in the supplied page, and the spending association is not proof that personalization caused the difference. The expectation gap is a reason to keep offers useful, clear, and easy to control. [1]\n\n### What the Lee’s evidence says—and does not say\n- Samantha Kim reported that about 85% of transactions were linked to loyalty accounts and that the rate had improved from the prior quarter. Her message says “as of Q2 2024,” but its timestamp is 1 July 2023. The period and denominator need to be reconciled. Transaction linkage is not the same as the share of shoppers with a complete profile or marketing consent. [4]\n- An internal document indexed as tmpec4ojx8n.docx reports that 45% of online customers engaged with personalized product recommendations and reports a 25% increase in average order value. The record gives no sample size, period, baseline, calculation method, or causal design, and the original filename/path is unavailable. Validate these figures against source systems before using them as performance targets. [5]\n- Another internal message mentions positive feedback on Lee’s online shopping platform, but it is anecdotal; the available material does not give a survey score or quantify satisfaction. [6]\n\n### Recommended action plan\n**Days 1–30: establish a trustworthy baseline.** Reconcile the transaction-link figure with POS, loyalty, e-commerce, and delivery records; define its period and denominator; and measure identification separately by channel and store. Verify the recommendation engagement and AOV figures, including their source, eligible population, exposure period, and calculation. Create a single baseline for conversion, AOV, gross margin, repeat purchase, redemption, and opt-outs. Confirm consent and preference controls before using customer-level data.\n\n**Days 31–60: build a small, customer-led pilot.** Select a few stores and online customers with enough eligible volume. Test two experiences:\n- personalized product recommendations or meal bundles based on the customer’s own recent purchases, browsing, and explicitly selected preferences;\n- a limited loyalty offer for a relevant complement or replenishment occasion, with inventory-aware rules for fresh produce, meat, seafood, bakery, and prepared food.\n\nLet customers choose language and any dietary or shopping preferences they want used. Base segments on observed shopping behaviour or explicit choices, not inferred ethnicity, health status, or other sensitive traits. Cap contact frequency and provide a simple way to change preferences or opt out.\n\n**Days 61–90: measure incrementality and decide what to scale.** Randomly assign eligible customers to a personalized treatment or a holdout receiving the usual experience. Keep timing and offer value comparable, and read results by channel and store. Make incremental gross margin per eligible customer the primary measure; also track conversion, AOV, items per basket, category attachment, redemption, repeat visits, stockouts/substitutions, and opt-outs or complaints. Scale only where the treatment beats the holdout after discount cost and operational effects; revise or stop offers that do not.\n\n### Management decision\nAuthorize a 90-day pilot design and baseline audit, then release campaign spend only after the data definitions, consent rules, control group, and margin measure are agreed. Public research suggests personalization is worth testing, and Lee’s internal records suggest the data and recommendation experience may already exist. The evidence does not justify promising a fixed percentage sales lift.\n\n### Sources\n[1] Deloitte, “Unlocking Customer Growth: Driving High Value Actions Through Personalization and Retail Media.” Full-page extraction was incomplete; publication date is not stated in the supplied material. https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/programs/chief-marketing-officer/articles/personalization-strategy-in-retail-media.html\n[2] Mark Burton, Richard Dawson, and Julie Jeancolas, dunnhumby, “Exploring the evolution of personalisation in grocery retail: Part One,” 14 June 2022. The article reports a Singapore shopper study conducted in early 2021. https://www.dunnhumby.com/articles/exploring-the-evolution-of-personalisation-in-grocery-retail/\n[3] Jeff Baskin, Supermarket News, “The intersection between loyalty, personalization, retail media and AI,” 2 June 2025. The cited Tesco example is not a quantified Lee’s outcome. https://www.supermarketnews.com/grocery-technology/the-intersection-between-loyalty-personalization-retail-media-and-ai\n[4] MatterMost-Sales Strategy-Sales Team-Samantha Kim; post timestamp 1 July 2023, message text says “as of Q2 2024.”\n[5] tmpec4ojx8n.docx, Enterprise API indexed filename for a personalization document; original filename and path unavailable.\n[6] MatterMost-Sales Strategy-Sales Team-David Kim; customer feedback on the online shopping platform, October 2023.\n",
